<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Want to Be a Doctor Because... – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Careers</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Want to Be a Doctor Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze the structure of various texts. Reading: Summarize the main points of various non-fiction texts. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by practicing minimal pair sounds.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze the structure of career-focused texts (e.g., "Read the job description and find the key duties."). Can summarize main points of texts related to career aspirations (e.g., "List two skills needed to be a doctor."). Can distinguish and correctly pronounce minimal pairs, demonstrating improved phonemic discrimination (e.g., /ʃ/ in "ship" versus /tʃ/ in "chip," and /dʒ/ in "joke" versus /tʃ/ in "choke").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a picture of a doctor helping a patient and asks students "What do you see?" and "What does a doctor do?". Students share ideas. The teacher then writes "doctor" on the board and asks "Why do people want to be a doctor?" to connect to the theme and activate prior knowledge related to career goals from Lesson 1.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from Lesson 1, specifically "doctor," "career," "plan," and "goals." The teacher also introduces and models the pronunciation of minimal pairs relevant to the lesson: /ʃ/ in "ship" and /tʃ/ in "chip," and /dʒ/ in "joke" and /tʃ/ in "choke," by saying them clearly and having students repeat chorally and individually. The teacher also models sentences like, "I want to be a doctor because I like helping others."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a doctor someone who teaches in a school? (No, a doctor helps sick people.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do doctors need a plan for their career? (Yes, they need to study and train.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'chip' the same sound as 'ship'? (No, different sounds: /tʃ/ and /ʃ/.)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Careers."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short non-fiction text (e.g., a brief article or blog post titled "A Day in the Life of a Young Doctor" or "How to Achieve Your Dream of Becoming a Doctor"). The text incorporates vocabulary such as <i>doctor, career, goals, plan, train, achieve, succeed, prepare, professional, skilled, important, qualifications, internship, practice</i>. It also uses grammar structures like "I want to be a doctor," "I will need to study hard," and "I would like to help people." The text is designed with clear headings and short paragraphs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Comprehend and analyze the main ideas and specific details within a short non-fiction text about a career path.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students read the text quickly and answer: "What is the main topic of this article?" and "Who is the article about?" (e.g., a doctor, someone planning to be a doctor).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read sentences related to the text and decide if they are True or False. Example: "To be a doctor, you only need to finish high school." (False) "Doctors need to train for many years." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match headings or sub-headings from the text to their corresponding main ideas provided in a jumbled list.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Analyzing text structure, summarizing main points, identifying details. Grammar: Wh-questions for career goals, future tense for plans ("I will need to..."), modals for intention and suggestions ("I would like to..."), "Want to + infinitive verb." Vocabulary: doctor, career, goals, internship, qualifications, plan, practice, train, achieve, succeed, prepare, professional, skilled, important. Phonemic Awareness: Minimal pairs /ʃ/, /tʃ/, /dʒ/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Gap-Fill Summary:</b> Students receive a worksheet with a short summary of the text from Stage 2, but with blanks for key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>career, qualifications, train, internship, succeed</i>) and grammatical structures (e.g., "want to be," "will need to"). Students fill in the blanks using a word bank, reinforcing both vocabulary and grammar in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Question & Answer Scan:</b> In pairs, students receive a list of Wh-questions (e.g., "What qualifications will you need?", "When will you start your internship?", "Why is it important to plan your career?"). They scan the original text to find and write down the answers. This activity encourages detailed reading and use of the target grammar and vocabulary. The teacher monitors for correct pronunciation of modals (should, would, will) and minimal pairs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Text Structure Analysis:</b> Students work in small groups to identify different parts of the text from Stage 2. They highlight or label: the main heading, sub-headings, introduction sentence, concluding sentence, and sentences that state qualifications needed. They discuss with their group members about how the text is organized, providing suggestions for improvement if they were to write a similar text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Careers."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Career Path Exploration (Small Group Discussion & Presentation Prep)</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups. Each group receives two short, different non-fiction texts (e.g., a "day in the life" of a lawyer and a "day in the life" of a teacher).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group reads both texts carefully, analyzing their structure and summarizing the main points of each career path. They will identify:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Key duties of each professional.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Qualifications or skills needed (e.g., <i>professional, skilled, important</i>).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">A sentence expressing why someone might *want to be* that professional.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After reading and analyzing, groups discuss: "What do you want to be in the future, a lawyer or a teacher, and why?" They must express their intentions using "I want to be a [career] because..." and "I would like to [verb]..." or "I will need to [verb]..." and incorporate vocabulary such as <i>plan, train, achieve, succeed, prepare</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group prepares a very short, informal summary to share with the class, highlighting key differences and similarities between the two careers, and stating their group's collective preference (if any) or individual intentions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individual students receive a new, very short paragraph (3-4 sentences) about a different career (e.g., an engineer or an artist). They must: 1) Identify the main idea of the paragraph. 2) List two skills or qualifications mentioned in the text. 3) Write one sentence explaining why someone might *want to* pursue this career, using "I want to + infinitive."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurately identifies the main idea of the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Lists relevant skills/qualifications from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "want to + infinitive" correctly to express intention.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>skills, qualifications, plan</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to correctly pronounce minimal pairs during oral parts of the activity.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned how to identify ________ in a text about careers."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I still need to practice summarizing texts about ________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about different careers did I find most interesting today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can analyzing text structure help me understand job descriptions better in the future?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The reading comprehension tasks were effective. Students successfully identified main ideas and specific details, and the text structure analysis helped them grasp organization.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students struggled with summarizing independently; additional scaffolding like sentence starters or key phrase banks could be useful. The minimal pair practice needs more focused repetition and listening discrimination exercises.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Connection to next lesson:** The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on the reading analysis by having students orally present their career aspirations and discuss the qualifications and plans needed, using the vocabulary and grammar from today's reading in interactive dialogues. We will specifically practice asking and answering "What do you want to be in the future?" and "I want to be a [career] because I like helping others."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>